 "appendix": [
  {
   "title": "Implementation notes and deviations",
   "html": "<ul><li><b>Judge model.</b> <code>gpt-5.4-mini-2026-03-17</code>, reasoning off, temperature 0, strict JSON schema, one input per call (the judge never sees other tokens, the trial, or the setting). We first tried <code>gpt-5.4-nano</code>; on 25 hand-made probe tokens it labeled incomplete words such as “pomegran” and “banan” as Items (4/25 errors vs 1/25 for mini, the one being a Fragment), so we switched before labeling any data.</li><li><b>Lens log-probabilities</b> are a log-softmax over the vocabulary after removing tokens with no letter or digit (the J++ readout filter), so baseline correction compares like with like.</li><li><b>Hold-phase and free-naming generation ran for all 516 trials in one pass,</b> before the readout block and K were fixed. Nothing in the hold phase or free naming depends on the block or K, and the main-trial lens data are not analyzed until both are frozen from the preliminary trials. Recognition (which needs the readout block) runs afterwards from the saved post-hold state.</li><li><b>Settings</b> were generated with <code>gpt-5.5</code> (5 thematic batches of 60 per category), screened for naming any specific item, food, animal, country, city, nationality or language, screened for category fit, deduplicated against each other and the preliminary settings, and 160 per category drawn with a fixed seed. Frozen before any hold-phase trial.</li><li><b>Carriers</b> are balanced: each of the 12 carriers is used 40 times in the main experiment and twice in the preliminary trials.</li><li><b>Hand-checks</b> of judge labels (step 0 validation) are done by Claude, not by a human; the sampled labels are listed on this page so they can be audited.</li><li><b>Fragment labels are noisy</b> (about 72% correct on a check of 47 Fragment labels: many short prefixes such as “ Aust” or “ Cri” are assigned to one country even though they begin several). The Fragment sensitivity analysis should be read with that in mind.</li></ul>"
  }
 ],
